<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul-Henri Spaak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul-Henri Spaak was a Belgian Socialist statesman, several times prime minister and foreign minister. He presided over the first General Assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1946, chaired the committee whose Spaak Report of 1956 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rome">Treaty of Rome</a>, and served as Secretary General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> from 1957 to 1961. He is counted among the founding fathers of European integration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-henri-spaak.jpg" alt="Paul-Henri Spaak" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-39998-0427,_Paul-Henri_Spaak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
